#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718151 :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/8f/8f473f1958f39173a66ded9caf1da1bb47efaeb0b3f2874b0763baf007603c4b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. The blue boundary and gray reference curves are concentric similar ellipses. Boundary crossing directions and tangencies shown by the arrows hold between the sampled points as well.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nEvery boundary vector of $\\mathbf F$ is tangent to the boundary, hence perpendicular to the outward normal. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n=0$ at every point, and its flux is zero.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/00/00d37bd512a3e54c0152f3a36eb85028ce26811e7b9f6221fed730a74ac2c209.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C=0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative. **false**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718152 :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/fb/fb36ae15e8e246c28658a4aec287b0e729af42f22a750a48985d2cdac052684a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and boundary $S$ shown above. $\\mathbf F$ expands horizontally and compresses vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The compression rate is three times the other rate.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf F$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. The compressing contribution is three times as large as the opposing contribution. There is net inflow, so the flux of $\\mathbf F$ is negative.\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/3b/3bfe8d84d85f76649e80e7a3d285454a4ee3fa08d9d7ac055d26fd31ec4bf845.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is negative. **true**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718165 :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/53/53f7c22a46869946a6ca502433b53ff18c5a08305ba2d7c0b6b70c003be6d40e.png)\n\nThe vector field is $\\mathbf F=\\mathbf U+\\mathbf V$. The panels show the two components separately at the same arrow scale, on the same boundary $S$. The field $\\mathbf U$ is uniform throughout the plane. The blue boundary and gray reference curves are concentric circles. Boundary crossing directions and tangencies shown by the arrows hold between the sampled points as well.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once counterclockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite small boundary arcs have equal lengths and opposite outward normals. The same field vector meets both arcs, so their normal contributions cancel in pairs. Thus $\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)=0$.\n\nThe field has motion along the boundary, but its arrows also cross consistently inward. Tangential motion contributes no flux. The normal component is negative all around the boundary, so the flux of $\\mathbf V$ is negative.\n\nThe normal component of a sum is the sum of its normal components. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_C(\\mathbf F)&=\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V)\\\\\n&=0+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/45/45032959530381081b44ac1039705a73b528f6c233219241e53d8665b4c8b0cf.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero. **false**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718167 :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/8d/8d83151eeb2676fe98c843d5875f966352da6302e81078c6f41a304f72cef4e9.png)\n\nThe vector field is $\\mathbf F=\\mathbf U+\\mathbf V$. The panels show the two components separately at the same arrow scale, on the same boundary $S$. The field $\\mathbf U$ is uniform throughout the plane. $\\mathbf V$ compresses horizontally and expands vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The expansion rate is three times the other rate.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite small boundary arcs have equal lengths and opposite outward normals. The same field vector meets both arcs, so their normal contributions cancel in pairs. Thus $\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)=0$.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf V$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. The expanding contribution is three times as large as the opposing contribution. There is net outflow, so the flux of $\\mathbf V$ is positive.\n\nThe normal component of a sum is the sum of its normal components. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_C(\\mathbf F)&=\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V)\\\\\n&=0+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/1c/1cc81aae3e10bd3d1f1b0636cc3bfc14cdedaf36077999311f690ac3ddb5de3f.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C>0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is nonzero. **true**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718178 :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/7a/7a7fde7df9d81a15cd7ad54827de8d8fa8bbcd62ea733abdfedab0fdead10f29.png)\n\nThe vector field is $\\mathbf F=\\mathbf U+\\mathbf V$. The panels show the two components separately at the same arrow scale, on the same boundary $S$. The field $\\mathbf U$ is uniform throughout the plane. $\\mathbf V$ compresses horizontally and expands vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The compression rate is three times the other rate.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite small boundary arcs have equal lengths and opposite outward normals. The same field vector meets both arcs, so their normal contributions cancel in pairs. Thus $\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)=0$.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf V$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. The compressing contribution is three times as large as the opposing contribution. There is net inflow, so the flux of $\\mathbf V$ is negative.\n\nThe normal component of a sum is the sum of its normal components. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_C(\\mathbf F)&=\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V)\\\\\n&=0+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/30/302e60144c8acf46ec5c68465fd56fbf67a0f006c1e407ee319c16b028d9c6c9.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C<0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not negative. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero. **true**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718191 :question/problem "![Vector fields and the directed boundary](images/fe/fe3a7c005ac9301125dfd6fa365f5c7c135940d53d5282d5422c720e88f150fe.png)\n\nThe vector field is $\\mathbf F=\\mathbf U+\\mathbf V$. The panels show the two components separately at the same arrow scale, on the same boundary $S$. The field $\\mathbf U$ is uniform throughout the plane. $\\mathbf V$ expands horizontally and compresses vertically, at speeds proportional to distance from the corresponding axis. The expansion and compression rates are equal.\n\nLet $C$ traverse $S$ once clockwise, and let $-C$ be the reversed traversal. In both cases use the **outward-pointing normal**.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply. Here *nonzero* means positive or negative; *not positive* includes zero, and *not negative* includes zero." :question/worked-solution "Flux measures net outward flow:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_C=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\nOutward normal components contribute positively; inward ones contribute negatively; tangential ones contribute zero.\n\nFor the uniform field $\\mathbf U$, opposite small boundary arcs have equal lengths and opposite outward normals. The same field vector meets both arcs, so their normal contributions cancel in pairs. Thus $\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)=0$.\n\nSplit $\\mathbf V$ into its horizontal and vertical flows. On the circle, pair equal-length small arcs related by a quarter turn. Their horizontal and vertical normal projections have matching geometric weights. Their normal contributions are equal and opposite, so they cancel. The flux of $\\mathbf V$ is zero even though the field crosses the boundary.\n\nThe normal component of a sum is the sum of its normal components. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi_C(\\mathbf F)&=\\Phi_C(\\mathbf U)+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V)\\\\\n&=0+\\Phi_C(\\mathbf V).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Field vectors and outward normals](images/89/894a06c475fd9611f4d335d5d26560ddd784b6c82f4fc23633607dbdb4a5a0a9.png)\n\nReversing traversal changes neither the outward normal nor arc length. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{-C}=\\Phi_C=0.\n$$\n\nEvaluate each statement:\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is nonzero. **false**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is not positive. **true**.\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive. **false**.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718199 :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/97/97e7f0082e187d8db6adee4a2f68972507d62389b6dc9ab5d306afce942cf0fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=\\left(- \\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $12$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/70/700d51dc84b25655363314549c35259857bd23a162526de15cd18d67e258456d.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=6\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-6\\le t\\le 6$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $- \\frac{1}{2}(6)=-3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-6}^{6}(-3)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(-3)(6-(-6))\\\\&=-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(-36)=-144.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718203 :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/97/97e7f0082e187d8db6adee4a2f68972507d62389b6dc9ab5d306afce942cf0fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(- x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/33/33b0d69e4eff448684f5fdabcc31ba2f48e183917878dc9ab72c3cb51e59584b.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=1\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-1\\le t\\le 1$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $-1(1)=-1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-1}^{1}(-1)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(-1)(1-(-1))\\\\&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(-2)=-8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718206 :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/a3/a3872da890615e96a775dd9d5245daf571783c7cdb0e20172b78025dc7744b49.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(4 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(4 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $4(2)=8$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(8)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(8)(2-(-2))\\\\&=32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(32)=128.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718208 :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/a3/a3872da890615e96a775dd9d5245daf571783c7cdb0e20172b78025dc7744b49.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(3 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(3 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $3(2)=6$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(6)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(6)(2-(-2))\\\\&=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(24)=96.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718210 :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/a3/a3872da890615e96a775dd9d5245daf571783c7cdb0e20172b78025dc7744b49.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(x)\\,\\mathbf i+(y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/33/33b0d69e4eff448684f5fdabcc31ba2f48e183917878dc9ab72c3cb51e59584b.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nOn the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=1\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-1\\le t\\le 1$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $1(1)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-1}^{1}(1)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(1)(1-(-1))\\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi=4(2)=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718219 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=2x-y+z$, where $x=s+t$, $y=s-t$ and $z=2s+t$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nHold $s$ fixed. The highlighted paths ending at $t$ give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, differentiate the outer function with respect to its intermediate variables:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=-1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, differentiate the intermediate variables with respect to $t$, treating $s$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}=-1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}=1.\n$$\n\nMultiply along each path and add:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}&=2(1)+(-1)(-1)+1(1)\\\\\n&=2+1+1\\\\\n&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718231 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x^2y-3yz$, where $x=st$, $y=s^2$ and $z=s\\sin t$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}$ in terms of $s$ and $t$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nHold $t$ fixed. The highlighted paths ending at $s$ give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s}.\n$$\n\nFirst, take the outer partial derivatives while holding the other intermediate variables fixed:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=2xy,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-3z,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=-3y.\n$$\n\nWrite these derivatives in terms of $s$ and $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=2(st)s^2=2s^3t,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=s^2t^2-3s\\sin t,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=-3s^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inner derivatives, with $t$ held fixed, are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}=t,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}=2s,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s}=\\sin t.\n$$\n\nIn particular, $\\sin t$ is constant when differentiating with respect to $s$. Now multiply along all three paths:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}&=(2s^3t)t+(s^2t^2-3s\\sin t)(2s)+(-3s^2)\\sin t\\\\\n&=2s^3t^2+2s^3t^2-6s^2\\sin t-3s^2\\sin t\\\\\n&=4s^3t^2-9s^2\\sin t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth the $x$ and $y$ paths contribute to the polynomial term. Both the $y$ and $z$ paths contribute to the sine term."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718234 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=e^{x+y+z}$, where $x=s-t$, $y=t^2$ and $z=st$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ in terms of $s$ and $t$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nHold $s$ fixed. The highlighted paths ending at $t$ give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nThe three outer partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=e^{x+y+z},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=e^{x+y+z},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=e^{x+y+z}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the intermediate variables gives the common factor $e^{s-t+t^2+st}$. With $s$ held fixed, the inner partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}=-1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}=2t,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}=s.\n$$\n\nSubstitute these into the chain rule and collect the common factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}&=e^{s-t+t^2+st}(-1)+e^{s-t+t^2+st}(2t)+e^{s-t+t^2+st}s\\\\\n&=(s+2t-1)e^{s-t+t^2+st}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718246 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(- 2 x + y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the bottom side $C_4$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_4}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the bottom side, outward points down, so $\\mathbf n=-\\mathbf j.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(- 2 x + y^{2})(0)+(2 y)(-1)\\\\\n&=- 2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $- 2 y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718254 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(- x y - 2 y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 x y - 3 x\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the top side $C_2$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the top side, outward points up, so $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf j.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(- x y - 2 y^{2})(0)+(2 x y - 3 x)(1)\\\\\n&=2 x y - 3 x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $2 x y - 3 x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718258 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(2 x y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(x y^{2} + 2 y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the right side $C_1$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the right side, outward points right, so $\\mathbf n=\\mathbf i.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(2 x y^{2})(1)+(x y^{2} + 2 y)(0)\\\\\n&=2 x y^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $2 x y^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718259 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(x + y\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 x\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the left side $C_3$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_3}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the left side, outward points left, so $\\mathbf n=-\\mathbf i.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(x + y)(-1)+(2 x)(0)\\\\\n&=- x - y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $- x - y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718267 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(- 2 x\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 x^{2} y + 3 y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. The outward flux across the left side $C_3$ is written as\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_3}\\boxed{\\phantom{x^2+y}}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "On the left side, outward points left, so $\\mathbf n=-\\mathbf i.$ The tangent component contributes zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n&=(- 2 x)(-1)+(2 x^{2} y + 3 y)(0)\\\\\n&=2 x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $2 x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718277 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(- x^{2} - 2 x\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(- 3 y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write its outward flux in the following order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{C_3}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_4}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_1}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "The outward normals on the right, top, left, and bottom sides are $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,-\\mathbf i,-\\mathbf j,$ respectively. Use the order in the supplied decomposition.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=x^{2} + 2 x\\qquad\\text{(left side)},\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf j)=- 3 y\\qquad\\text{(top side)},\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=3 y\\qquad\\text{(bottom side)},\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf i)=- x^{2} - 2 x\\qquad\\text{(right side)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the ordered quadruple is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(x^{2} + 2 x,\\ - 3 y,\\\\&\\quad 3 y,\\ - x^{2} - 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nThese are integrands on different sides; opposite signs alone do not cancel their integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718285 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(2 x\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(x y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write its outward flux in the following order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{C_4}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_1}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "The outward normals on the right, top, left, and bottom sides are $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,-\\mathbf i,-\\mathbf j,$ respectively. Use the order in the supplied decomposition.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=- x y\\qquad\\text{(bottom side)},\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf j)=x y\\qquad\\text{(top side)},\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=- 2 x\\qquad\\text{(left side)},\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf i)=2 x\\qquad\\text{(right side)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the ordered quadruple is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- x y,\\ x y,\\\\&\\quad - 2 x,\\ 2 x\\bigr)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nThese are integrands on different sides; opposite signs alone do not cancel their integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718288 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(3 y\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(- 2 x y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write its outward flux in the following order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{C_4}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_1}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_3}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "The outward normals on the right, top, left, and bottom sides are $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,-\\mathbf i,-\\mathbf j,$ respectively. Use the order in the supplied decomposition.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=2 x y^{2}\\qquad\\text{(bottom side)},\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf j)=- 2 x y^{2}\\qquad\\text{(top side)},\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf i)=3 y\\qquad\\text{(right side)},\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=- 3 y\\qquad\\text{(left side)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the ordered quadruple is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(2 x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2},\\\\&\\quad 3 y,\\ - 3 y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nThese are integrands on different sides; opposite signs alone do not cancel their integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718295 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\nConsider $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\bigl(x y^{2}\\bigr)\\mathbf i+\\bigl(2 x y^{2} - x y\\bigr)\\mathbf j.$ The rectangle $C$ has right, top, left, and bottom sides $C_1,C_2,C_3,C_4,$ respectively. Write its outward flux in the following order.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{C_3}g_1\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}g_2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_1}g_3\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_4}g_4\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich ordered quadruple $(g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4)$ gives the four dot products? Leave expressions in terms of $x$ and $y,$ before substituting side equations. Do not evaluate any integral." :question/worked-solution "The outward normals on the right, top, left, and bottom sides are $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,-\\mathbf i,-\\mathbf j,$ respectively. Use the order in the supplied decomposition.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_1&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf i)=- x y^{2}\\qquad\\text{(left side)},\\\\\ng_2&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf j)=2 x y^{2} - x y\\qquad\\text{(top side)},\\\\\ng_3&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(\\mathbf i)=x y^{2}\\qquad\\text{(right side)},\\\\\ng_4&=\\mathbf F\\cdot(-\\mathbf j)=- 2 x y^{2} + x y\\qquad\\text{(bottom side)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the ordered quadruple is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\bigl(- x y^{2},\\ 2 x y^{2} - x y,\\\\&\\quad x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2} + x y\\bigr)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nThese are integrands on different sides; opposite signs alone do not cancel their integrals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718314 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Every component has continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. For each field $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$, compare $P_y$ and $Q_x$ as functions on the entire plane.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, $P=xe^{xy}+\\cos(x-y)$ and $Q=ye^{xy}-\\cos(x-y)$. Differentiating with respect to the specified variables gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=x^2e^{xy}+\\sin(x-y),\\\\Q_x&=y^2e^{xy}+\\sin(x-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheir difference is $(x^2-y^2)e^{xy}$. This vanishes at the origin, but at $(1,0)$ it equals $1$. The equality is not an identity, so $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, $P=ye^{xy}+\\cos(x-y)$ and $Q=xe^{xy}-\\cos(x-y)$. Now both prefactors depend on the differentiation variable, so use the product rule as well as the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=e^{xy}+xye^{xy}+\\sin(x-y),\\\\Q_x&=e^{xy}+xye^{xy}+\\sin(x-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese agree everywhere. Hence $\\mathbf G$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, $P=2x\\sin(y^2)+y$ and $Q=2x^2y\\cos(y^2)+x$. We obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=4xy\\cos(y^2)+1,\\\\Q_x&=4xy\\cos(y^2)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese also agree everywhere, so $\\mathbf H$ is conservative.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf G$ and $\\mathbf H$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718319 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply.\n\nFor $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ with continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$, the field is conservative exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere. The partial derivatives for the fields below are supplied:\n\n| Field | $P_y$ | $Q_x$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\mathbf F$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $\\mathbf G$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n| $\\mathbf H$ | $0$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "All three fields have polynomial components, so their first partial derivatives are continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$. We can therefore apply the test $P_y=Q_x$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, $P=x+2y$ and $Q=2x-y$. Thus $P_y=2=Q_x$ everywhere, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, $P=x+y$ and $Q=y-x$. Here $P_y=1$ while $Q_x=-1$. These are unequal, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, $P=x^2+1$ does not depend on $y$, and $Q=y^2+3$ does not depend on $x$. Hence $P_y=0=Q_x$ everywhere, so $\\mathbf H$ is conservative. The components themselves do not need to be equal.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$ and $\\mathbf H$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718321 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, continuous first partial derivatives and the identity $P_y=Q_x$ characterize conservative fields. All components here are polynomials, exponentials or trigonometric functions, so the continuity condition holds everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, $P=y^2+x$ and $Q=2xy+y$. We get\n\n$$\nP_y=2y,\\qquad Q_x=2y.\n$$\n\nThe derivatives agree everywhere, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, $P=e^{x-y}+y$ and $Q=-e^{x-y}-x$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\nP_y=-e^{x-y}+1,\\qquad Q_x=-e^{x-y}-1.\n$$\n\nThe derivatives differ by $2$, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, both components are $\\cos(x-y)$, but the relevant derivatives are\n\n$$\nP_y=\\sin(x-y),\\qquad Q_x=-\\sin(x-y).\n$$\n\nThese agree at some points but not everywhere. For example, at $(\\pi/2,0)$ they are $1$ and $-1$. Thus $\\mathbf H$ is not conservative.\n\nSelect only $\\mathbf F$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718341 :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/c1/c12715040b1be9b8b40c43a35f1659c2f401c42c71806a75059e071f3dc64756.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(x + 2)\\,\\mathbf i+(y + 3)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nThe full field does not have equal side fluxes. Decompose it into radial and uniform parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U\\\\\\mathbf R&=(x)\\,\\mathbf i+(y)\\,\\mathbf j\\\\\\mathbf U&=(2)\\,\\mathbf i+(3)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, the two fluxes add. The uniform part has opposite signed contributions on opposite sides of equal length:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=(2)(4)+(3)(4)-(2)(4)-(3)(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow compute the radial part $\\mathbf R$. On the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $1(2)=2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(2)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(2)(2-(-2))\\\\&=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(8)=32.\n$$\n\nAdding the uniform part gives $\\Phi=32+0=32$. As a direct check, the full field gives:\n\n| Side | Normal component | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $4$ | $16$ |\n| Top | $5$ | $20$ |\n| Left | $0$ | $0$ |\n| Bottom | $-1$ | $-4$ |\n\nThe four signed contributions sum to $32$. Only the radial part has four equal contributions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718343 :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/65/6523762415fc82242d906be9839387f66aa41490690f573bffe71cfb07924fd5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(2 x + 2)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y + 5)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nThe full field does not have equal side fluxes. Decompose it into radial and uniform parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U\\\\\\mathbf R&=(2 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y)\\,\\mathbf j\\\\\\mathbf U&=(2)\\,\\mathbf i+(5)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, the two fluxes add. The uniform part has opposite signed contributions on opposite sides of equal length:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=(2)(4)+(5)(4)-(2)(4)-(5)(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow compute the radial part $\\mathbf R$. On the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $2(2)=4$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(4)(2-(-2))\\\\&=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(16)=64.\n$$\n\nAdding the uniform part gives $\\Phi=64+0=64$. As a direct check, the full field gives:\n\n| Side | Normal component | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $6$ | $24$ |\n| Top | $9$ | $36$ |\n| Left | $2$ | $8$ |\n| Bottom | $-1$ | $-4$ |\n\nThe four signed contributions sum to $64$. Only the radial part has four equal contributions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718349 :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/31/31cefa640f819acfa55e823e6ef9fec59871f4d9cd646099ef8b15d63909e69c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(- 2 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(1 - 2 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $2$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/33/33b0d69e4eff448684f5fdabcc31ba2f48e183917878dc9ab72c3cb51e59584b.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nThe full field does not have equal side fluxes. Decompose it into radial and uniform parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U\\\\\\mathbf R&=(- 2 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- 2 y)\\,\\mathbf j\\\\\\mathbf U&=(0)\\,\\mathbf i+(1)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, the two fluxes add. The uniform part has opposite signed contributions on opposite sides of equal length:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=(0)(2)+(1)(2)-(0)(2)-(1)(2)=0.\n$$\n\nNow compute the radial part $\\mathbf R$. On the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=1\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-1\\le t\\le 1$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $-2(1)=-2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-1}^{1}(-2)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(-2)(1-(-1))\\\\&=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(-4)=-16.\n$$\n\nAdding the uniform part gives $\\Phi=-16+0=-16$. As a direct check, the full field gives:\n\n| Side | Normal component | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $-2$ | $-4$ |\n| Top | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| Left | $-2$ | $-4$ |\n| Bottom | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe four signed contributions sum to $-16$. Only the radial part has four equal contributions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718350 :question/problem "![Vector field and square](images/ea/eaa690b15fb3324ad446c96544ae6aa0d992d40c8d47eaa6ceefbf9d96c1771c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=(x - 2)\\,\\mathbf i+(y + 3)\\,\\mathbf j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$, centered at the origin and with sides parallel to the coordinate axes. Calculate the total outward flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$. Enter the exact value.\n\n{{flux}}" :question/worked-solution "The outward flux is\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\sum_{k=1}^{4}\\int_{C_k}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_k\\,\\mathrm ds.\n$$\n\n![Sides and outward normals](images/e4/e41a7d6c424bccdec3c9b196d93e9bd7c81d70ed139c257a7afb850fc3527ab4.png)\n\nThe right, top, left and bottom sides have outward unit normals $\\mathbf i$, $\\mathbf j$, $-\\mathbf i$ and $-\\mathbf j$, respectively.\n\nThe full field does not have equal side fluxes. Decompose it into radial and uniform parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U\\\\\\mathbf R&=(x)\\,\\mathbf i+(y)\\,\\mathbf j\\\\\\mathbf U&=(-2)\\,\\mathbf i+(3)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf R+\\mathbf U)\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\mathbf R\\cdot\\mathbf n+\\mathbf U\\cdot\\mathbf n$, the two fluxes add. The uniform part has opposite signed contributions on opposite sides of equal length:\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf U}=(-2)(4)+(3)(4)-(-2)(4)-(3)(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow compute the radial part $\\mathbf R$. On the right side $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r(t)=2\\,\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j$ for $-2\\le t\\le 2$. Then $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i$ and $\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|=1$, so $\\mathrm ds=\\mathrm dt$. The radial normal component is $1(2)=2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi_1&=\\int_{-2}^{2}(2)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=(2)(2-(-2))\\\\&=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuarter-turn symmetry preserves both the radial field and the outward normal component, so all four radial contributions are equal. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{\\mathbf R}=4(8)=32.\n$$\n\nAdding the uniform part gives $\\Phi=32+0=32$. As a direct check, the full field gives:\n\n| Side | Normal component | Flux |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Right | $0$ | $0$ |\n| Top | $5$ | $20$ |\n| Left | $4$ | $16$ |\n| Bottom | $-1$ | $-4$ |\n\nThe four signed contributions sum to $32$. Only the radial part has four equal contributions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718356 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\mathbf F=\\bigl(a^2xy^2+(a+1)^2y\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i+(4x^2y+x)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if and only if $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nDifferentiating gives\n$$\nP_y=2a^2xy+(a+1)^2,\\qquad Q_x=8xy+1.\n$$\nThe identity $P_y=Q_x$ becomes\n$$\n(2a^2-8)xy+\\bigl((a+1)^2-1\\bigr)=0\n$$\nfor every $x$ and $y$. Thus the coefficients must satisfy both\n$$\na^2=4\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad (a+1)^2=1.\n$$\nThe first condition gives $a^2=4$, hence $a=2$ or $a=-2$. The constant-term condition eliminates $a=2$, because $(2+1)^2=9\\ne1$. Solving only the constant-term condition would instead give $a=0$ or $a=-2$; $a=0$ fails $a^2=4$. Both identities must hold for the same constant.\n\nFor $a=-2$, we instead obtain\n$$\nP_y=8xy+1=Q_x\n$$\neverywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$. Therefore $a=-2$. The positive square root satisfies only one of the two necessary conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718361 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\mathbf F=(2ay+x^2)\\,\\mathbf i+(5-3x+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if and only if $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nDifferentiating the first component with respect to $y$ and the second with respect to $x$ gives\n$$\nP_y=2a,\\qquad Q_x=-3.\n$$\nThus\n$$\n2a=-3,\\qquad a=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\nAt $a=-\\frac{3}{2}$, both partial derivatives equal $-3$ everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718372 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\mathbf F=(a^2xy^2+x^2)\\,\\mathbf i+(4x^2y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a positive constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if and only if $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\nP_y=2a^2xy,\\qquad Q_x=8xy.\n$$\nTheir equality must hold for every $x$ and $y$:\n$$\n2(a^2-4)xy=0.\n$$\nIn particular, at $(x,y)=(1,1)$ this gives $a^2=4$. Thus $a=2$ or $a=-2$. The prompt states that $a$ is positive, so only $a=2$ is allowed.\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ into the derivatives gives\n$$\nP_y=8xy=Q_x\n$$\neverywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$. Therefore $a=2$. A negative square root also satisfies the derivative identity, but fails the stated sign condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718382 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=- x \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}$ and $y=2 x^{3}$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x$. Assume $2 x^{3} + x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is independent and $y$ depends on $x$, add the contributions along both paths.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nHold the other variable fixed in each partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- \\frac{x}{x + y} - \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- \\frac{x}{x + y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $y=2 x^{3}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- \\frac{x}{2 x^{3} + x} - \\ln{\\left(2 x^{3} + x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- \\frac{x}{2 x^{3} + x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the dependency:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=6 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nMultiply along the indirect path, then add the direct path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=- \\frac{x}{2 x^{3} + x} - \\ln{\\left(2 x^{3} + x \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(- \\frac{x}{2 x^{3} + x}\\right)\\left(6 x^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{6 x^{3}}{2 x^{3} + x} - \\frac{x}{2 x^{3} + x} - \\ln{\\left(2 x^{3} + x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718391 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=- 2 \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}$ and $y=2 x^{2}$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x$. Assume $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is independent and $y$ depends on $x$, add the contributions along both paths.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nHold the other variable fixed in each partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- \\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- \\frac{2 y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $y=2 x^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- \\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{4} + x^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- \\frac{4 x^{2}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{4} + x^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the dependency:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4 x.\n$$\n\nMultiply along the indirect path, then add the direct path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=- \\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{4} + x^{2}}} \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(- \\frac{4 x^{2}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{4} + x^{2}}}\\right)\\left(4 x\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{16 x^{3}}{\\sqrt{4 x^{4} + x^{2}}} - \\frac{2 x}{\\sqrt{4 x^{4} + x^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718397 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=- \\cos{\\left(x^{2} + y \\right)}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{x}$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x$. Assume $x\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is independent and $y$ depends on $x$, add the contributions along both paths.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nHold the other variable fixed in each partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=2 x \\sin{\\left(x^{2} + y \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\sin{\\left(x^{2} + y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $y=\\frac{1}{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=2 x \\sin{\\left(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\sin{\\left(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the dependency:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\frac{1}{x^{2}}.\n$$\n\nMultiply along the indirect path, then add the direct path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=2 x \\sin{\\left(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)} \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(\\sin{\\left(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &=2 x \\sin{\\left(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)}}{x^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718398 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2 \\ln{\\left(x + y \\right)}$ and $y=e^{- x}$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x$. Assume $x + e^{- x}>0$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is independent and $y$ depends on $x$, add the contributions along both paths.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nHold the other variable fixed in each partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{2}{x + y}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{2}{x + y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $y=e^{- x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{2}{x + e^{- x}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{2}{x + e^{- x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the dependency:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- e^{- x}.\n$$\n\nMultiply along the indirect path, then add the direct path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=\\frac{2}{x + e^{- x}} \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(\\frac{2}{x + e^{- x}}\\right)\\left(- e^{- x}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{2}{x e^{x} + 1} + \\frac{2}{x + e^{- x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718412 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2 y \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 4 \\right)}$ and $y=x^{2} + 4$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ in terms of $x$. Assume $x^{2} + 4>0$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ is independent and $y$ depends on $x$, add the contributions along both paths.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nHold the other variable fixed in each partial derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{4 x y}{x^{2} + 4}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=2 \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 4 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $y=x^{2} + 4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=4 x, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=2 \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 4 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the dependency:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=2 x.\n$$\n\nMultiply along the indirect path, then add the direct path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}&=4 x \\\\ &\\quad +\\left(2 \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 4 \\right)}\\right)\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\ &=4 x \\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 4 \\right)} + 4 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718425 :question/problem "![](images/45/45468822eed304a0075a5aba689657fa397bb95cce81af2cf94c873043941629.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=x\\mathbf i+y\\mathbf j$. Let $C$ be the quarter of the unit circle in the first quadrant traversed from $A=(0,1)$ to $B=(1,0)$, and let $-C$ denote the same curve traversed from $B$ to $A$.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The line integral is the total work done by the field along the oriented path. A force component along the motion contributes positive work; a component against the motion contributes negative work; a perpendicular force contributes zero work.\n\nThe field points radially outward. Motion along the circle is tangent to it, so the force is perpendicular to the direction of motion at every point, even though the force itself is nonzero.\n\n![](images/f5/f55460ef335cf7e23862e7e78be8aaba01174aace3ddae50ba43448140d2a117.png)\n\nEvery local contribution to work is zero. Hence $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. The statement that this integral is negative is false; the statement that it is zero is true.\n\nReversing the direction changes the tangent's direction but preserves perpendicularity.\n\n![](images/bf/bffdd0261970f5ef6779684e61e504be66fe924f378f3027d2ff316729498168.png)\n\nThus $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$, so the statement that the reversed integral is positive is false.\n\nSelect only $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718442 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c9cbb706a8c7d474db9a845312fb8549839f9018dae60f01fb3f558d7c2c62c.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(1+y)\\mathbf j$. Let $C$ be the upper unit semicircle traversed from $A=(-1,0)$ through $T=(0,1)$ to $B=(1,0)$, and let $-C$ denote the same curve traversed from $B$ to $A$.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The line integral is the total work done by the field along the oriented path. A force component along the motion contributes positive work; a component against the motion contributes negative work; a perpendicular force contributes zero work.\n\nThe force points upward everywhere on the semicircle. From $A$ to $T$ the particle rises, so the field does positive work. From $T$ to $B$ it descends, so the field does negative work.\n\n![](images/24/24d2dc1405f1bffea52f5a00757d7ee1a801bb1b5b7b7605190ef056a387d42f.png)\n\nTo decide the total, pair a small upward displacement on the left half with the reflected downward displacement at the same height on the right half. The vertical displacements have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. Because the force magnitude is $1+y$, the paired forces have equal magnitudes too. Their work contributions cancel. Horizontal displacement contributes no work because the force is vertical.\n\nThis pairing covers the whole semicircle, giving $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. This is cancellation of positive and negative work, rather than perpendicularity throughout the path. Thus the negative-integral statement is false and the zero-integral statement is true.\n\nOn $-C$, the right half is traversed upward and the left half downward; the same pairs still cancel.\n\n![](images/26/26bc03a3cdd95cff5eaaacbf57d5afd2101ecd58c193cc2fa1a229be78cdb40b.png)\n\nEquivalently, $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$. The statement that the reversed integral is positive is false.\n\nSelect only $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$."}]}
